ely to have more than a  10-minute conversation with a Canadian  they were less knowledgeable about  Canadian events and so forth so you take  these two together China is well perhaps  or has been for the last 10 years the  largest source country of immigrants to  Canada so the first question is you know  should immigrants be required to learn  English well I think they should because  citizenship is really all about  membership in a society you can't be  separate from the society because you're  you're involved you're pay taxes you're  part of the medical system the school  system you're part of that society you  live beside other people you work with  other people you you can't just be  isolated in your own language community  I mean you can but from the point of  view of the receiving community the  existing society here that's probably  not considered very desirable you know  if I listen to what Canadians have to  say like my teammates in the old-timers  hockey you know they say one of them  said he was at a citizenship swearing-in  ceremony and there were people there who  couldn't speak English and he was  appalled that we would give citizenship  to someone who can't speak English and  so surely the existing members of  society should have some say in who they  want to accept as new citizens and I am  sure that a requirement to speak one or  one of the two national languages would  be quite high so I think that's a  legitimate I request unfortunately the  level the government has asked for is  extremely low you will not be able to  work with a beginner level in English  but I guess it's better than nothing but  even at that there's this great  resistance to it  at the other end of the spectrum you  have this study of group of I think 30  or so and I'll post the link Mandarin  speakers who'll essentially made no  progress we spend a fortune here on  language instruction for immigrants and  immigrants may spend some money of their  own they certainly spend their time some  time the time they spend in class and  the Mandarin speaking group doesn't  improve why is that well in obviously  the Mandarin speakers have certain  disadvantages when learning English they  don't have as much common vocabulary the  Slavic language speakers have almost  Latin and Greek origin words this the  language systems are more similar  however is someone who has learned Asian  languages I don't consider that to be an  insurmountable problem rather the  biggest issue is that the Slavic  speakers are more motivated to find out  about the new society that they have  moved to even though they maintain  contact with the you know in many cases  with the Russian speaking or Ukrainian  speaking or I don't know what they just  said Slavic maybe our Croatian speaking  community but they integrate more  they're more motivated to connect with  and make friends with other members of  this new society that they moved into  whereas the motivation for this amongst  matter speakers is not very strong and  that's been my observation you do meet  exceptions I met people who came here  and went to work selling door-to-door  when they have a very strong accent and  lo and behold within a few years they  were speaking flawless English so  there's no reason why a Chinese person  if so motivated would also not be able  to improve their English but it's  absolutely astounding this is a very  serious study after seven years there's  no significant progress in their level  of English that can only mean to me that  they're not motivated to improve I am  thoroughly convinced that anyone who is  motivated to improve who takes  responsibility for their own learning  will improve but there again I think the  Mandarin speaking group is more inclined  to believe that a teacher has to teach  them they have to go to school and that  there's nothing they can do  their own and they don't sort of relate  the fact that they watched nothing but  mandarin-speaking television programs in